## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design guardrails early in prompt pipelines, focusing on simple heuristics, mitigations, and verifiable safety.

## Key Concepts

- Risk scoring from prompts
- PII and system-prompt leakage detection
- Mitigations: redaction, output limits, guardrails
- Guarded prompts and verification

## Code Example

```javascript
function riskScore(prompt) {
  let score = 0;
  if (/\b(email|e-mail|phone)\b/i.test(prompt)) score += 3;
  if (/\b(export|download|CSV|exported)\b/i.test(prompt)) score += 2;
  if (prompt.toLowerCase().includes("system prompt")) score += 4;
  return Math.min(score, 10);
}
function mitigate(prompt) {
  return prompt
    .replace(/[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\.[A-Za-z]{2,}/g, "[REDACTED_EMAIL]")
    .replace(/\b\d{3}[-.\s]?\d{3}[-.\s]?\d{4}\b/g, "[REDACTED_PHONE]");
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for multi-tenant workloads with different policy baselines?
- What tests would you add to validate redactions without affecting legitimate outputs?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design scalable, privacy-aware prompt routing with safety checks under latency constraints, a common challenge in enterprise LLM deployments.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant-aware routing
- Privacy-preserving sanitization
- Latency-budget guided model selection
- Guardrails and veto logic
- Pluggable sanitizer architecture

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal gating prototype (JS)
class Gate {
  constructor() {}
  route(prompt, tenant) {
    const risk = {
      privacy: tenant.privacyRisk || 0,
      safety: tenant.safetyRisk || 0,
      latency: tenant.latencyBudget || 100
    };
    const model = risk.latency < 60 ? 'lite' : 'standard';
    const template = risk.privacy > 7 || risk.safety > 7 ? 'guarded' : 'default';
    return { tenant: tenant.id, model, template };
  }
}
const g = new Gate();
console.log(g.route('Prompt', {id: 'tenantA', privacyRisk:8, safetyRisk:5, latencyBudget:40}));
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test guardrails under adversarial prompts?
- How would you scale to thousands of tenants with dynamic policies?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a scalable, secure prompt pipeline that enforces per-tenant policies, blocks prompt-injection, and records privacy-preserving audits in real time.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant policy schemas (allowed_actions, data_classifications)
- Safety shims against system-prompt leakage and prompt-injection
- Privacy-preserving reasoning traces in audit logs
- Latency budgeting and streaming guarantees

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype components for the three stages
function validatePolicy(prompt, tenantPolicy) {
  // check allowed_actions and data_classifications
  const okAction = tenantPolicy.allowed_actions.includes('execute') || prompt.includes('export');
  const safeData = !/SECRET|PASSWORD/i.test(prompt);
  return okAction && safeData;
}

function guardPrompt(prompt, policy) {
  // simple sanitizer to remove system prompts or injected cues
  let p = prompt.replace(/system prompt:.*$/i, '').trim();
  return p;
}

function logAudit(input, modelVersion, latency, reasoning) {
  return {
    input: input, modelVersion, latency, reasoningTrace: reasoning
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this against non-determinism and ensure consistent audits at scale?
- What metrics would you collect to detect policy drift or leakage risk over time?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests ability to design a lightweight, repeatable prompt-testing workflow and catch prompt drift early.

## Key Concepts
- Prompt versioning
- Drift metrics (embedding similarity, content guards)
- Reproducibility and logging

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype skeleton for drift check
function driftFlag(basePrompt, paraphrase) {
  const baseOut = model(basePrompt)
  const paraOut = model(paraphrase)
  const sim = cosineSimilarity(embed(baseOut), embed(paraOut))
  return sim < 0.8
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this for multi-language prompts?
- How would you integrate with CI to prevent drift before deploy?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a modular, auditable routing layer for prompts with data-sensitivity and reproducibility concerns. It emphasizes concrete signals, edge-case handling, and a small prototype to demonstrate flow control.

## Key Concepts

- Prompt routing signals: intent, data_sensitivity, reproducibility
- Edge-case handling: ambiguity, missing sources, conflicting data
- Lightweight prototyping: minimal module map and payload schema

## Code Example

```javascript
function routePrompt(prompt) {
  const text = prompt.toLowerCase()
  const toLiterature = /(summarize|literature|research)/.test(text)
  const toExperiment = /(experiment|plan|propose)/.test(text)
  const needsAudit = /(audit|trace|reproducible)/.test(text)

  if (toLiterature && toExperiment && needsAudit) {
    return {
      modules: ['Literature-Summary', 'Experiment-Plan', 'Audit-Log-Formatter'],
      payload: { prompt, sources: [], trace: { confidence: 0.8 } }
    }
  }
  // Additional routing logic...
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates ability to design a simple, realistic prompt router with multilingual support, a common constraint in enterprise tooling at Citadel and Cloudflare.

## Key Concepts

- Language detection
- Intent routing
- Locale-aware payloads
- Edge-case handling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal router sketch (pseudo)
function route(prompt){
  const lang = detectLang(prompt);
  const intent = classify(prompt);
  const module = selectModule(intent, lang);
  return {module, payload: buildPayload(module, prompt, lang)};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this with mixed-language prompts?
- How would latency constraints influence the design?
